{"type": "FeatureCollection", "features": [{"type": "Feature", "properties": {"company": "Oregon Trunk Railway", "line": "Oregon Trunk: Wishram \u2013 Bend", "id": "oregon-trunk-railway", "color": "#1E6091", "mode": "intercity_rail", "avg_speed_kmh": 35, "speed_source": "estimated for a steam-era mountain-grade line climbing ~3,500 ft over 152 miles through the Deschutes River canyon; unsourced", "segments": [{"start": 1911, "end": 1921, "headway_min": 720, "note": "Seattle builder W.F. Nelson incorporated the Oregon Trunk Railway in 1906 to build up the Deschutes River canyon toward central Oregon, but James J. Hill (Great Northern/Spokane, Portland & Seattle) bought the company in 1909 and put his top engineer, John F. Stevens, on the route. From mid-1909 Hill's crews and E.H. Harriman's rival Des Chutes Railroad (Union Pacific) built parallel grades up opposite banks of the narrow Deschutes canyon in one of railroading's most notorious construction races -- workers on the two lines reportedly traded gunfire and sabotaged each other's blasting. Hill drove a golden spike at Bend on Oct 5 1911, and through passenger service Wishram-Bend began Nov 1 1911, following the Deschutes River, then Willow Creek through Pelton to Madras, and on via the Crooked River Gorge to Redmond and Bend. Frequency estimated, unsourced.", "sources": ["NOT SOURCED AT SEGMENT LEVEL: the references below cover this line, but nothing has been checked specifically against this segment's start year, end year or service. Treat the dates and the headway as unverified. Supporting references for the line:", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Oregon_Trunk_Line"]}, {"start": 1921, "end": 1958, "headway_min": 480, "note": "In 1921 the Oregon Trunk abandoned its own grade between South Junction and Metolius and began using the parallel ex-Des Chutes Railroad (Harriman/UP) track instead -- the losing side of the construction race turned out to have the better line on that stretch. Full consolidation of the two railroads' trackage was completed in two phases by 1935. The line was extended south to Chemult in 1926 to connect with the Southern Pacific, and into California to reach the Western Pacific in 1931, making Bend part of a real north-south through corridor for a few decades. Regular passenger service (by the end just a single coach on the rear of a freight) lasted until 1958. Frequency estimated, unsourced.", "sources": ["NOT SOURCED AT SEGMENT LEVEL: the references below cover this line, but nothing has been checked specifically against this segment's start year, end year or service. Treat the dates and the headway as unverified. Supporting references for the line:", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Oregon_Trunk_Line"]}]}, "geometry": {"type": "LineString", "coordinates": [[-120.94334, 45.65133], [-120.93975, 45.64932], [-120.93503, 45.64408], [-120.93093, 45.64196], [-120.92566, 45.63677], [-120.91511, 45.63029], [-120.91148, 45.62467], [-120.90736, 45.62069], [-120.90667, 45.61943], [-120.90668, 45.61787], [-120.90765, 45.61511], [-120.91329, 45.6103], [-120.91423, 45.609], [-120.91482, 45.60711], [-120.91462, 45.60482], [-120.91298, 45.60236], [-120.90983, 45.60003], [-120.9005, 45.59607], [-120.89865, 45.59127], [-120.89837, 45.58824], [-120.90019, 45.58427], [-120.90494, 45.57978], [-120.90655, 45.57731], [-120.90773, 45.57238], [-120.90772, 45.56887], [-120.91109, 45.56044], [-120.91036, 45.55815], [-120.90085, 45.5484], [-120.89876, 45.54192], [-120.89573, 45.54021], [-120.88868, 45.53928], [-120.88681, 45.53829], [-120.88623, 45.53736], [-120.88575, 45.53113], [-120.88484, 45.52834], [-120.88399, 45.52734], [-120.88085, 45.52562], [-120.87485, 45.52506], [-120.8662, 45.52745], [-120.8636, 45.52748], [-120.86214, 45.52691], [-120.85435, 45.52096], [-120.83859, 45.51545], [-120.82959, 45.51519], [-120.82789, 45.51409], [-120.82742, 45.51221], [-120.82876, 45.51059], [-120.83485, 45.50813], [-120.83699, 45.50679], [-120.83863, 45.50509], [-120.8397, 45.50283]